  "content": {
    "id": "p27-18f-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Show that there are two possible triangles $\\triangle ABC$ for which $a=5$, $c=10$ and $\\angle A=20^{\\circ}$. Find the two possible measures of $\\angle C$, correct to the nearest degree.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\angle C=43^{\\circ}$ or $137^{\\circ}$.\n\nThe sine rule gives supplementary solutions; both leave a positive third angle.",
      "worked": "By the sine rule,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{\\sin C}{10}&=\\frac{\\sin20^{\\circ}}{5}\\\\\\sin C&=2\\sin20^{\\circ}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nLet $C_1$ be the acute solution. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}C_1&=\\sin^{-1}(2\\sin20^{\\circ})\\\\C_1&\\approx43.1602^{\\circ}\\\\C_2&=180^{\\circ}-\\sin^{-1}(2\\sin20^{\\circ})\\\\C_2&\\approx136.8398^{\\circ}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nBoth solutions satisfy $20^{\\circ}+C<180^{\\circ}$, so both give a positive third angle and hence a possible triangle. Therefore, $\\angle C=43^{\\circ}$ or $137^{\\circ}$, to the nearest degree.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
